<div class="page-header">
  <h1 class="page-header__title">Why HelloDeploy Exists</h1>
  <p class="page-header__subtitle">
    Deploying a web application usually takes more work than writing it.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="card__body">
    <p>
      Before a website is reachable, someone has to deal with servers, application processes,
      ports, a reverse proxy, certificates, domains, DNS records, environment variables, and a
      repeatable way to publish updates. None of that is the application you set out to build.
    </p>
    <p>
      HelloDeploy exists to make those steps easier to manage — not to hide them. The aim is a
      guided deployment workflow that still shows you what is happening behind your
      application, so that when something breaks you can tell what broke and why.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is built for students learning how deployment works, developers publishing personal
      projects, freelancers managing client applications, educators teaching web systems, teams
      with no dedicated operations staff, and website owners who would rather not administer a
      server.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card mt-8">
  <div class="card__body">
    <h2>Make deployment easier to understand</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      Reducing complexity is not the same as hiding it. A platform that deploys your
      application but leaves you unable to explain a failure has traded one problem for
      another. HelloDeploy tries to make these things legible:
    </p>
    <ul class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">
      <li>what your application needs before it can run in production</li>
      <li>what happens, in order, during a deployment</li>
      <li>why a deployment failed, and at which stage</li>
      <li>how a domain connects to a running application</li>
      <li>what HTTPS does and what it depends on</li>
      <li>how configuration and secrets are kept separate from your code</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card mt-8">
  <div class="card__body">
    <h2>Built through real deployments</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      HelloDeploy is developed against real projects rather than hypothetical ones. The
      failures that shape it are ordinary and specific: a build that works locally and fails in
      a container, a missing environment variable, an application listening on the wrong port, a
      DNS record pointing at the wrong place.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      Where those problems generalise, they become documentation and guides instead of staying
      in a private changelog.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card mt-8">
  <div class="card__body">
    <h2>Where the project stands</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      HelloDeploy is an active pilot, operated by Henson Sagorsor as an MIT capstone project and
      running on shared infrastructure. It is free to use while in this stage.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      That has practical consequences worth stating plainly: supported runtimes are still being
      verified one at a time, features and availability may change, resource allowances are set
      by hand rather than bought, and this is not a service to put a business-critical
      application on without understanding those limits. The
      <a href="/terms">Terms</a> cover this in more detail, and
      <a href="/supported-runtimes">Supported Runtimes</a> lists what has actually been tested.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

